{
  "subtype": "what_if",
  "outcome_variable": "hardware_incident_rate",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "incident_category",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "Distinguishes hardware from other categories; the query is about hardware incidents, so this scoping variable is implied by the query text. Not a settable lever."
    },
    {
      "name": "failure_mode",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "Identifies the specific hardware failure causes (connectivity, drive failure, overheating, etc.). This is exactly the 'most common failure causes' the query proposes to address. However, a failure mode is a diagnostic classification of what went wrong, not itself a directly settable lever; addressing it requires an unspecified upstream intervention."
    },
    {
      "name": "recurrence_signal",
      "causal_role": "outcome",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Whether an incident recurs is a downstream manifestation of the incident rate itself, not a common cause of treatment and outcome. Behaves as an outcome/proxy rather than a confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "affected_scope",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Scope (individual vs production vs shared) plausibly influences both which failure modes occur and the incident rate/visibility, and is not named in the query. Weak-to-moderate confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "software_issue_subtype",
      "causal_role": "other",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Pertains to software incidents, largely NULL for hardware; irrelevant to the hardware outcome and not a common cause of the hardware failure treatment."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["failure_mode"],
  "treatment_intervenable": false,
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "affected_scope", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.4}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.25,
  "rationale": "The added columns identify the 'treatment' (failure_mode = the common hardware failure causes) and scope it to hardware, matching the query's framing. But the what-if claim fails on both prongs. (a) failure_mode is a diagnostic label of what failed, not an intervenable lever an actor can set/change/decide; the query's 'addressed' action (e.g., a maintenance policy, hardware replacement program, patch schedule) is nowhere represented as a settable treatment. (b) No genuine confounder that is a common cause of BOTH an intervention and the hardware incident rate is provided: incident_category and failure_mode are query-implied scoping/treatment, recurrence_signal is an outcome/proxy, software_issue_subtype is irrelevant, and only affected_scope is a plausible not-named confounder — and even it is weak and not clearly a common cause of an intervention. The set does not set up an identifiable counterfactual effect of an intervenable treatment."
}

